A stop stops its caller rather than the thread its capability names,
so a supervisor that stops a child stops itself, and the child runs on:
a thread stops by its holder.

Caught-by: fuzz fuzz-pmp8 fuzz-rp2350 fuzz-smp2 fuzz-work test qemu-replay
Report: a thread stopped that no stop named, nor its own fault nor the loss of its process

diff --git a/kernel/syscall.c b/kernel/syscall.c
--- a/kernel/syscall.c
+++ b/kernel/syscall.c
@@ -508,7 +508,7 @@ static int op_thread(struct thread *t, const struct cap *cap, uint32_t op, uint3
         if ((arg[1] | arg[2] | arg[3]) != 0) {
             return KERR_INVALID_ARG;
         }
-        arg[1] = sched_stop(target);
+        arg[1] = sched_stop(t);
         return KERR_OK;
     case OP_THREAD_FAULT:
         /* The frame keeps what the trap saved, and only a fault leaves the thread stopped with it. */
